SUMMARY:🌍 [W2] Post X thread EN — i18n Autopilot
DESCRIPTION:Clique pour tweet #1 (prérempli) :\nhttps://twitter.com/inten
 t/tweet?text=i18n%20bug%20of%20the%20week%3A\n\n━━━ Thread complet (relis
  avant\, paste #2-#5 en replies) ━━━\n\n1/ i18n bug of the week:\n\n  "gr
 eeting": "Welcome\, {name}!"\n  → "Bienvenue\, {nom} !"\n\nLooks fine. Co
 mpiles. Tests pass. Ship to prod.\n\nResult: every French user sees liter
 al "Bienvenue\, {nom} !" with braces. Because the templating engine looks
  for {nom}\, your code interpolates {name}.\n\n2/ The bug: a translator (
 human or LLM) treated {name} as a word.\n\nIt's not a word. It's code. Sa
 me class:\n\n  "count": "{{count}} items"        // {{count}} accidentall
 y → {nombre} \n  "html":  "Click <a>here</a>"       // <a> tag closes wro
 ng\n  "icu":   "{count\, plural\, one {…}}"  // ICU breaks if {count} ren
 amed\n\n3/ The fix: validate placeholders are 1:1 BEFORE merging.\n\n  be
 foreKey: {name}\, {{count}}\, <a>\n  afterKey:  {name}\, {{count}}\, <a>\
 n  → diff matches: ✅\n  → diff differs: ❌ reject\n\nThat's most of the en
 gineering. Translation is the easy part.\n\n4/ My Action does this in CI:
 \n- Diff locale files\n- Find missing keys\n- AI-translate\n- Validate pl
 aceholders intact\n- Commit back to PR\n\nIf validation fails\, no commit
 . No broken French.\n\n5/ Free MIT GitHub Action:\n\n→ github.com/marketp
 lace/actions/i18n-autopilot\n\nWhat's your worst i18n bug in prod?\n\n━━━
 ━━━━━━━━━━━━━\nRepo : https://github.com/marketplace/actions/i18n-autopil
 ot
